WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.072
This report combines two different kinds of reasoning in one result.

00:00:04.072 --> 00:00:12.702
For contact information, we normalize blank-looking text and then use COALESCE to choose the first usable

00:00:12.702 --> 00:00:15.042
contact method. That is a fallback problem.

00:00:15.042 --> 00:00:21.497
For billing, we use CASE because we are classifying values into meaningful categories.

00:00:21.497 --> 00:00:24.652
A missing bill is labeled Needs review rather than converted to zero.

00:00:24.652 --> 00:00:28.942
An exact zero can be labeled No Charge.

00:00:28.942 --> 00:00:32.642
Then the higher billing tiers are applied using ordered thresholds.

00:00:32.642 --> 00:00:40.257
This is a good example of why NULL should not always be treated the same way across columns.

00:00:40.257 --> 00:00:45.692
For contact information, falling back to another field may be perfectly reasonable.

00:00:45.692 --> 00:00:52.027
If email is missing but mobile phone is present, we can still create a useful display value.

00:00:52.027 --> 00:00:56.292
For billing, a missing amount may indicate incomplete data.

00:00:56.292 --> 00:01:01.092
Replacing it with zero could falsely imply that the patient owes nothing.

00:01:01.092 --> 00:01:04.297
The same pattern appears in business analytics.

00:01:04.297 --> 00:01:09.807
A missing product description can fall back to a short title, but a missing product price should probably not

00:01:09.807 --> 00:01:12.122
become zero.

00:01:12.122 --> 00:01:18.837
A missing office phone can fall back to mobile, but missing salary should not become zero automatically.

00:01:18.837 --> 00:01:22.882
Use COALESCE when a fallback is semantically appropriate.

00:01:22.882 --> 00:01:28.021
Use CASE when missingness itself should produce a distinct decision or category.
